## AC-SO-08 · the two callers
1. `run_loop` runs the check on every entry it records (the dashboard starts it via `start_daemon_thread`).
2. `python3 queue_daemon.py check-logs` calls `find_missing_gate_logs()`. Run read-only against the live state now, it reports `ORCH-STDOUT-1: 38 recorded route(s) without a readable log (38 no-log-recorded)`.

## Consequences (D-S7CORE13-03)
1. **A new log file per route.** Every daemon-fired route now writes `logs/<log_subdir>/orch-<stem>-<ts>.log`, next to `toni-<stem>-<ts>.log`. There is no rotation. The largest file the tests produced was 328 210 bytes (the 320 KiB case). A typical real route should be about 6 KB, plus any `print` output and tracebacks.
2. **Entries gain `log_file`.** Entries recorded before this merge don't have it and never will.
3. **`check-logs` fails on the existing history by design.** It will report every older entry until they drop out of the capped lists: the last 30 for `completed`, up to 15 for `failed`. A `no-log-recorded` on an entry dated after the merge means a regression; any other reason means a disk problem.
4. **I did not restart the live daemon.** It's the process running this route (`python3 -u queue_daemon.py`, PID 1457330), and a restart would have killed it. It keeps the old code until you restart it. The first route after the restart is where AC-SO-02 turns green in production.
5. **Crash reasons are now kept.** A crashing route's uncaught traceback lands in its log, so the next `exit_code: 1` has a reason on disk. The 8 existing failed entries never will.
6. **`python3 -u` changes timing only.** The child's `print()` output reaches the file line by line instead of block by block, which is also why output printed before a timeout kill survives (the timeout test checks this). Nothing else about the command changed.
